The strong part is:

No giant hallucinated vulnerability report.

QDW only labels something VERIFIED if a safe, authorized reproduction/evidence check supports it.

Bitsec's existing sandbox, inference-proxy and anti-cheat mechanisms are excellent donors for that architecture.

This should stay strictly defensive/authorized, but as a product that's potentially much easier to charge for than generic AI infrastructure.

There are also ways to bootstrap without spending much

Two current programs are worth your attention.

Nosana has an ongoing decentralized-AI builder/hackathon program with compute credits, explicitly intended to get developers building on its GPU network.

Akash's ecosystem grants explicitly fund open-source tooling, infrastructure and interfaces around Akash. A Dell adapter/compute-comparison layer is much more aligned with that mandate than inventing another chatbot. Heurist's developer program also provides free development credits in the thousands-of-credits range for developers, which could provide another inference source for bootstrapping your probes and products.

I would exploit those as builder resources, not speculate on their tokens.

My ranking for you specifically
Dell Live Compute API / Radar — smallest path from code you already have to something somebody can consume.
Bittensor Reality API — extremely natural extension and a nice niche with little conceptual overhead.
Apply Dell/QDW to Akash + Nosana developer programs — possible funding/compute without needing customers first.
Verified repo-security API using Bitsec mechanisms — potentially higher-value customer, slightly more work.
Agent Harness Index from Ridges/Bitsec/Harnyx — strategically excellent for QDW.
Full Moltwork / external agent marketplace — cool later, but not where I'd spend scarce effort today.

